  "query_preview": "(Today through Sunday)...\nIssued at 253 AM EDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nA deep cut off upper-level low currently sits over the Mid-Atlantic \nwith a split flow jet over the Plains. Ridging and surface high \npressure prevail over Indiana. Large-scale subsidence will allow \nclear skies to continue today and tomorrow.\n\nTemperatures will be modulated by boundary layer processes, with a \nlarge spread between highs and lows anticipated. Efficient mixing \nwill allow highs to rise above normal into the mid/upper 70s. Lows \nhowever will be a bit below normal due to efficient radiative \ncooling, with values into the 40s.\n\nLight winds from the surface high pressure should create good \nconditions for patchy fog during the overnights, especially this \nmorning and Tuesday morning. The strong solar insolation will \ndissipate any fog quite quickly after sunrise. Dew points during the \nafternoon hours both today and tomorrow are expected to be below \nguidance, due to efficient boundary layer mixing.\n\nAn upper-level trough is moving on shore over southern California, \nwhich is expected to move eastward becoming cut off from the polar \njet. The low then ingests moisture from Hurricane Polo, while \ninducing cyclogenesis over eastern Colorado. As suspected in \nprevious updates, guidance has slowed the progression of the upper-\nlow and resulting surface system. Rain chances now begin increasing \non Thursday rather than Wednesday. Some members of guidance now show \nrain arriving as late as Friday.\n\nSurface high pressure slides eastward on Wednesday, allowing winds \nto become southwesterly. With rainfall delayed a few days, warm air \nadvection and partly sunny skies will bring much warmer temperatures \nto the region. Highs in the 80s appear likely both Wednesday and \nThursday, with some locations over southern Indiana approaching 90. \nFriday appears cooler due to the aforementioned rainfall finally \narriving, with highs in the 60s/low 70s. Should the system slow \nfurther, then these may be revised upward with future forecast \nupdates.\n\nRegardless, ensembles show a more progressive pattern developing \nwith the upper-low and its associated cold front pushing eastward \nover weekend. Cooler than average temperatures follow, with \nlingering rain chances as the front moves eastward rather slowly. A \nsecondary wave developing along the front is possible, and is shown \nby a few models. Model consensus diverges considerably so confidence \nin any one solution is low at this time. \n\nDays 8-14: Ensemble guidance hints at large-scale troughing taking \nhold and persisting well into October. This would promote an \nextended period of cooler than normal temperatures.",
